From 2cfcc3386c587bbbef1745cdc19f5a1109941932 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 04:00:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Batch: FBar formulation by kernels
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The gradients, the inner forces and the stiffness matrices of the FBar
behaviour integrators (isotropic and orthotropic, 3D and plane strain) are
computed by kernels (batch::FBarData, src/Batch/FBar*.cxx):

- the deformation gradients at the center of the elements are computed
  with the integration rule of order 0 of their geometry, an additional
  rule of the kernels (AbstractBehaviourIntegrator::
  getAdditionalIntegrationRulesForKernels), whose derivatives of the
  unknowns and geometric data are computed with the ones of the rules of
  the elements;
- the kernel of the standard deformation gradients is followed by a kernel
  replacing F by F̄ = α F, α = (J0/J)^(1/3) (1/2 and F̄33 = 1 in plane
  strain), F and α being kept;
- the inner forces are the ones of the standard finite strain formulation;
- the stiffness matrices are the ones of the standard formulation with the
  tangent operator K' = α K + (K F) ⊗ dα/dF, plus a matrix of rank one per
  element a ⊗ b, a being the inner forces of the pseudo-stress
  α/(ie J0) K F and b the derivative of J0 with respect to the unknowns of
  the element: assembled matrix, matrices element by element, diagonal and
  action without assembly. The tangent operator K' is computed once after
  each integration of the behaviour.

The helpers of the computations by kernels shared by the standard and FBar
behaviour integrators (kernels data, tangent operator blocks, inner forces)
move to BehaviourIntegratorBase. The regularisation of Faltus et al. is
still refused.

Tests: BatchIntegrationTest compares the FBar formulation by kernels to the
integration point by point (isotropic and orthotropic, 3D and plane
strain, with and without prediction, assembled and without assembly,
generic, openmp, stlpar and cuda behaviours; host, debug device, GPU, two
processes): relative differences from 1e-15 to 1e-12; seven mutations
detected. GradientsKernelsTest no longer expects the refusal of FBar.
